all health experts  stress the importance of receiving the  flu shot  and practicing proper hygiene to help  ensure children don't catch covid19  or anything else jeff semple global news  toronto going the distance for a good  cause coming up a military veteran steps  into a new mission  against kovid 19.  security was high at a paris courthouse  today for the start of the charlie hebdo  terror trial 14 people are accused of  being accomplices in the attack on the  satirical newspaper's offices  in 2015 and a shooting spree at a kosher  supermarket two days later  three gunmen were killed in police raids  and the attacks left 17 people dead  including several cartoonists at charlie  hebdo the publication was targeted after  it published caricatures of the prophet  muhammad  something considered offensive in islam  the newspaper  republished those images in this week's  edition  pope francis held his first public  audience in six months today and he  declared friday as a day of prayer  and fasting for lebanon  nearly 500 catholics gathered in a  vatican courtyard instead of saint  peter's square  everyone wore masks a lebanese priest  held his country's flag  as the pope called for peace and  dialogue in lebanon  plunging population still ahead a  troubling report on at-risk species  in canada  canada may be a nature lover's paradise  but new research shows  some of our wildlife is in serious  decline natural habitats are  increasingly under threat  that's having a major impact on survival  according to the world wildlife  federation between 1970 and 2016  populations of species assessed as at  risk have fallen an average of 59  percent  and species deemed globally at risk have  plunged by an average of  42 percent in canada robin gill looks at  the decline of these vulnerable species  and what's being done to stop it  in british columbia's interior  volunteers at this bird sanctuary have  been working  diligently to build underground homes  for the burrowing owl  in the 1990s this migratory bird  population  which likes to tunnel dropped by 90  percent  considered endangered there are only  roughly 270 left  the largest issue still eludes this and  that is  having enough owls returning into  our into british columbia such that they  can sustain their population over time  without our interference  a new report from the world wildlife  fund highlights the plight of at-risk  animals  their numbers dwindling in canada i  think it's a growing  urgency knowing that we're seeing these  continued downward trends  there are other animals at risk bare and  ground caribou  also known as reindeer have seen some  herds declined by as much as  98 percent because natural resource  companies are moving in  and destroying their habitats north  atlantic right whales are critically  endangered  only 411 exist in the world largely  because they keep getting killed by  ships or fishing gear  things are not going in the right  direction you know not only are species  declining but  how we are addressing the  nature of the economy that gives rise to  all of these  stressors that we know are problems for  now  projects like the burrowing owl  conservation society have to exist  to monitor their existence the vicious  circle  that humans have to interfere in  protecting a problem  we created robin gill global news  vancouver  next looking back at the second world  war and how it shaped  the present day  99 year old george marco has a lot to  celebrate tonight the second world war  veteran just finished his final lap  of a 100 kilometer mission marco has  been doing laps around his ontario  seniors  residence since april it's his way of  raising money to fight kovid 19.  but he's still a little short of his  goal to raise 100  000 dollars so he plans to keep doing  laps  until he reaches his target he is one of  tens of millions of people who took part  in the second world war  the global conflict lasted six years and  it was the most destructive and  deadliest in history  that war impacted virtually every part  of the world including canada  it formally ended 75 years ago today  though as eric sorenson explains it  sparked changes that remain  to this day from the rise of hitler to  the attack on pearl harbor  it was war that spanned the globe it saw  cities and civilians firebombed  it produced genocide it introduced  nuclear annihilation  more than 70 million people were killed  about 4 percent of the entire world  canada was deeply affected close to 100  000 were killed or wounded  more than a million served in the forces  and countless more  helped the war effort on farms in  factories and in fundraising  canada fought around the world we fought  in the far east we fought in hong kong  we fought in sicily in italy in his book  the fight for history tim  cook says canada's role has not been  adequately chronicled  over the years we forgot about our  contribution  our service and our sacrifice we didn't  do a good job in telling our stories the  formal end of the war the signing of  surrender documents by japan 75 years  ago  set off enormous and enduring change  worldwide for better or worse  the creation of the united nations it  was hoped would bring countries together  to resolve differences  but new boundaries sparked the cold war  and ongoing conflicts  ever since this titanic conflict that  forever changed the world that we live  in that saw the rise of  superpowers saw the cold war saw the  decolonization of many empires  that were still with us today canada  emerged from the war with stronger ties  to the economy and the military of the  united states  but we also forged a canadian path  forward in peacekeeping  and as an influential power in  international trade and diplomacy  and at home emphasis on social inclusion  from pensions for returning veterans  to strengthening public education and  ultimately  universal health insurance the war  stimulated government intervention into  the lives of canadians there was the  baby bonus  uh there was a increased urbanization  there was industrialization we began to  step out onto the world stage that we  saw ourselves as part of the world  community and a force for good  of which we were canada's population  today is triple what it was in 1945.  spurred by immigration a country that  has come of age  striving to be a prosperous and  multicultural model to the world  eric sorensen global news toronto  and that is global national for this  wednesday i'm donna friesen tonight's  your canada is buck lake in alberta  southwest of edmonton  we'd love to see your canada please  email it to viewers at  globalnational.com  thanks for watching it's good to be back  hope to see you here again tomorrow  take care of yourselves bye-bye
 

